Close each file that the hexdump -s skip passes over entirely.
When the skip covered a whole regular file, do_skip() passed over it
without reading, and next() then opened the following file over the
FILE pointer of the skipped one without closing it. Every wholly
skipped file leaked a stream, so with a few hundred files ahead of the
skip point the C runtime ran out of streams: the later files failed to
open, were reported as errors and were not dumped. Close the skipped
file before opening the next one.

---
 libbb/dump.c |    5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

--- a/libbb/dump.c
+++ b/libbb/dump.c
@@ -400,6 +400,11 @@
 			do_skip(dumper, fname ? fname : "stdin");
 		if (dumper->pub.dump_skip == 0)
 			return 1;
+#if ENABLE_PLATFORM_MINGW32
+		/* skipped the whole file */
+		if (dumper->fd != stdin)
+			fclose(dumper->fd);
+#endif
 	}
 	/* NOTREACHED */
 }
